  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nGoing through Wednesday All is generally on track with the \nforecast and no significant adjustments have been made so far today.\nthe heat wave continues as strong mid/upper ridging builds across\nthe region. High temperatures will again approach the triple \ndigits in many areas today and tomorrow, with high temp records in\njeopardy at multiple local climate sites. One notable difference \nfrom recent days is a shift to easterly low level flow, gradually \nbringing a drier airmass into the area. This will continue to yield\nlower heat indices across a portion of east central MS today and \nfor most areas east of I-55 on Wednesday. A Heat Advisory remains \nin effect today for all other areas, and another another has been\nissued for tomorrow in the same area. With deep layer moisture \nbecoming more limited to the southern and western portions of the \narea, that is the region where diurnal convection will be more \nlikely this afternoon - south/west MS, northeast LA, and \nsoutheast AR, and we have begun to see a few showers as of early\nthis afternoon. By tomorrow, the focus will be even more limited \nto south MS and central LA. A few of these storms could produce \nlocally strong wind gusts. \n\nThursday through next Tuesday: A drier airmass will continue to\nshift into the area Thursday into Friday. This airmass will still\nsupport hot days, with temps still approaching the triple digits \nin the ArkLaMiss Delta through Saturday. But with dewpoints in \nthe 60s, mixing down into the 50s at times, heat stress will \nsignificantly decrease through the end of the week. Morning temps \nwill also dip into the 60s at times in many areas late this week.\nDeep layer moisture will begin to increase again this weekend \ninto early next week, with returning chances for diurnal \nconvection each day. /DL/EC/",
